Prototypes;

g. distinguish clearly among calculated, simulated, bench-demonstrated,
independently validated, certified, and operationally proven results;

h. avoid representing projections, simulations, assumptions, estimates, or
design targets as measured physical performance;

i. use the Project Materials only for the duration reasonably necessary to
complete the permitted Evaluation; and

j. discontinue use immediately if continued Evaluation would create an
uncontrolled risk to people, property, infrastructure, or the environment.

6. EVALUATION PROTOTYPES

6.1. Evaluation Prototypes are permitted only for internal Evaluation and
must not be sold, leased, transferred, delivered to a customer, used to
fulfill a contract, placed into service, or Operationally Deployed.

6.2. Evaluation Prototypes must not use improvised pressure vessels,
improvised compressed-gas storage, improvised hydrogen storage, unverified
oxygen-service components, defeated protective devices, or components
operated outside their documented ratings.

6.3. Any activity involving hydrogen, oxygen enrichment, pressure, high
current, high voltage, concentrated chemicals, contaminated water, rotating
machinery, thermal extremes, or stored energy must be performed only by
persons competent to identify and control the associated hazards.

6.4. Evaluation Prototypes must be de-energized, depressurized, purged,
rendered non-operational, securely retained for further permitted Evaluation,
or responsibly dismantled when the Evaluation ends.

6.5. Permission to construct an Evaluation Prototype is not a representation
that the design is complete, safe, certified, code compliant, suitable for a
particular purpose, or capable of achieving any stated target.

7. BATTERY-FREE REPRESENTATION

For purposes of the Project, "battery-free" means that the reference
architecture does not rely on electrochemical battery storage.

The term does not mean that the Project creates energy, operates without an
energy source, violates conservation laws, provides unlimited endurance, or
eliminates the need for storage.

The reference architecture may use externally supplied or locally harvested
energy, segmented supercapacitor storage, thermal storage, conditioned
electrical buses, and produced chemical energy.

You must not use the term "battery-free" in a false, misleading, or
thermodynamically unsupported manner.

